BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART:20260915T220000Z
DTEND:20260915T233000Z
DTSTAMP:20260915T000000Z
SUMMARY:The American Revolution at 250: Historians Discuss the Many Worlds of 1776
DESCRIPTION:\n\n\n\n \n\nThe American Revolution at 250: Historians Discuss 
 the Many Worlds of 1776\n\n\n\nIn conjunction with The Declaration of 
 Independence Display we invite you to join us for a special 
 event!\n\nFamously\, in December 1773\, over a hundred colonists snuck onto 
 ships in Boston Harbor after nightfall\, destroying chests of tea worth 
 nearly $2 million today.  Often portrayed as a local tax protest that 
 helped spark a Revolution\, this event raises all kinds of questions: How 
 did others elsewhere in America and across the world see such an affair? 
 Why were the protesters dressed as Native Americans? Where did all that tea 
 come from in the first place? \n\n The “Boston Tea Party” was just one 
 example of the many ways in which the American Revolution was a global 
 phenomenon\, whose causes and consequences reached from India and China to 
 Indigenous nations across the North American continent. Join us as Duke 
 historians Adriane Lentz-Smith and Philip Stern launch a year of 
 discussions on these many different “Worlds of 1776\,” in conversation 
 with UNC historian Kathleen DuVal\, Pulitzer Prize winning author of Native 
 Nations: A Millennium in North America.\n\n \n\nBrought to you by Duke We 
 the People: A Duke & Durham Initiative\; the John Hope Franklin Humanities 
 Institute\; and the Duke University Department of History’s 2026-2027 
 program on the “Worlds of 1776.”\n\nSponsored by the Triangle Early 
 American History Seminar\n\n\n\n\n\n \n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nSpeaker bios 
 alphabetically by last name:\n\nKathleen DuVal\n\nKathleen DuVal is the 
 Carl W. Ernst Distinguished Professor of History at the University of North 
 Carolina at Chapel Hill\, specializing in early America and cross-cultural 
 interactions among Native American\, European\, and African populations. 
 She is the author of several acclaimed books\, including Native Nations: A 
 Millennium in North America\, winner of the 2025 Pulitzer Prize in 
 History\, and Independence Lost: Lives on the Edge of the American 
 Revolution. A frequent contributor to national publications like The 
 Atlantic and the New York Times\, she also appeared in the Ken Burns 
 documentary The American Revolution.\n\n \n\nAdriane Lentz-Smith\n\nAdriane 
 Lentz-Smith is an Associate Professor of History and Senior Fellow at the 
 Kenan Institute for Ethics at Duke University. Her research focuses on 
 African American history\, twentieth-century U.S. history\, and the history 
 of the United States in a global context. She is the author of Freedom 
 Struggles: African Americans and World War I and is currently working on 
 Afterlives: Sagon Penn\, State Violence\, and the Twilight of Civil 
 Rights\, an exploration of state power\, civil rights\, and white supremacy 
 following the Voting Rights Act of 1965.\n\n \n\nPhilip Stern\n\nPhilip 
 Stern is a Professor of History at Duke University\, where he teaches and 
 writes on the intellectual\, political\, economic\, and legal history of 
 the British Empire. He is the author or editor of four books\, including 
 The Company-State\, awarded the American Historical Association's Morris D. 
 Forkosch Prize\, and Empire\, Incorporated: The Corporations that Built 
 British Colonialism\, winner of the 2024 Stansky Prize. His research has 
 been supported by major fellowships from the National Endowment for the 
 Humanities and the Andrew W. Mellon Foundation.\n\n\nRead With Us\n\nUS 
 Constitution/Declaration of Independence Childrens' Booklist \n\nUS 
 Constitution/Declaration of Independence Adult Booklist \n\n\n\nFull Durham 
 Schedule\n\nTo maximize access across the region\, the documents are hosted 
 in two Durham spaces:\n\n\n	Durham County Main Library: September 8 – 
 September 20\, 2026\n	Nasher Museum of Art at Duke University: September 
 24\, 2026 – January 3\, 2027 - click here for more 
 information\n\n\n\n\n\nThe Declaration of Independence is organized in 
 collaboration with Duke Arts and the Nasher Museum of Art and is part of We 
 the People: A Duke & Durham Initiative that marks the 250th anniversary of 
 the signing of the Declaration of Independence through a series of public 
 events examining the nation’s evolving identity. Three historic copies of 
 the Declaration of Independence are on loan from the David M. Rubenstein 
 Americana Collection. Leadership support for We the People has been 
 generously provided by David M. Rubenstein. \n\n\nInformation on Entering 
 Main Library\n\nParking is available in the library parking lot or at the 
 North Queen Street Garage located at 115 North Queen Street which is behind 
 the apartments directly across the street from the library.
LOCATION:Main Auditorium\, Main Library (300 N. Roxboro St)
ORGANIZER;CN="Celeste Holtzmann":MAILTO:choltzmann@dconc.gov
CATEGORIES:Announcements, Author Visit, Classes & Lectures, Durham, Humanities, Law, Literacy, North Carolina Collection
UID:LibCal-14288-17454675
URL:https://durhamcountylibrary.libcal.com/event/17454675
END:VEVENT
