SUMMARY:Zine Workshop: Notes on Freedom
DESCRIPTION:Zines are small\, self or independently published booklets that 
 give creators a space to share ideas\, stories\, perspectives\, and art 
 outside of traditional publishing. Zines usually emphasize personal voice\, 
 experimentation\, and storytelling. Zines have also been used as a way for 
 people to share their own stories about art\, community\, personal lives\, 
 political expressions\, and criticisms. In commemoration of America 250 and 
 Duke’s “We the People” initiative\, join us in the Makerlab for a 
 zine workshop that examines freedom. \n\nHow do we imagine freedom through 
 text and visuals? What does it look like when we try to articulate how it 
 surfaces in our lives\, our communities\, and our history?  In this Notes 
 on Freedom workshop\, we’ll think critically and creatively about the man 
 meanings of freedom. We’ll articulate the possibilities\, the belonging\, 
 the contradictions\, the resistance and the hope that comes with freedom. 
 No previous zine making experience is needed! \n\n \n\n \n\n
LOCATION:MakerLab\, Main Library (300 N. Roxboro St)
ORGANIZER;CN="Dahlia Bullock":MAILTO:dahbullock@dconc.gov
CATEGORIES:Arts & Crafts, Community Engagement, Writing
